> Simple examples

>> Four predictions determine every count

Truth (0,0,1,1), predictions (0,1,1,1) give rows (1,1) and (0,2).
Accuracy is 3/4; positive-class precision is 2/3, recall one, F1 4/5 and IoU 2/3.
For class zero, precision is one and recall 1/2. Macro F1 is the mean of the two
class F1 values, not the harmonic mean of macro precision and macro recall. We also
show the effect of declaring a third class absent from both truth and predictions.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/metrics_counts.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<std::int64_t> truth = {0, 0, 1, 1}, prediction = {0, 1, 1, 1};
  dm::nn::ConfusionMatrix         cm(2), expanded(3);
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < truth.size(); ++i) {
    cm.add(truth[i], prediction[i]);
    expanded.add(truth[i], prediction[i]);
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  std::vector<double>    cls, precision, recall, f1, iou, support;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < 3; ++i) {
    const auto m = expanded.per_class(i);
    cls.push_back(i);
    precision.push_back(m.precision);
    recall.push_back(m.recall);
    f1.push_back(m.f1);
    iou.push_back(m.iou);
    support.push_back(m.support);
  }
  t.add_column("class", cls);
  t.add_column("precision", precision);
  t.add_column("recall", recall);
  t.add_column("f1", f1);
  t.add_column("iou", iou);
  t.add_column("support", support);
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.bars({0, 1, 2}, {cm.accuracy(), cm.macro().f1, expanded.macro().f1}, "score", {37, 99, 235}, .5)
      .title("Macro scores depend on the declared classes")
      .x_label("0 = accuracy, 1 = two-class F1, 2 = three-class F1")
      .y_label("ratio")
      .y_limits(0, 1.2);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(950, 430).save_svg("build/metrics_counts.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\nAccuracy: {:.6f}; two-class macro F1: {:.6f}; three-class macro F1: {:.6f}.\n",
      t.to_md(6),
      cm.accuracy(),
      cm.macro().f1,
      expanded.macro().f1);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| class | precision | recall | f1 | iou | support |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 1.000000 | 0.5 | 0.666667 | 0.500000 | 2 |
| 1 | 0.666667 | 1.0 | 0.800000 | 0.666667 | 2 |
| 2 | 0.000000 | 0.0 | 0.000000 | nan | 0 |


Accuracy: 0.750000; two-class macro F1: 0.733333; three-class macro F1: 0.488889.
\image(figures/metrics_counts.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Class-specific counts expose precision/recall tradeoffs and the effect of an absent configured class.)
\alttext(A table gives three per-class summaries, including undefined IoU for the absent class. Bars compare accuracy and macro F1 under two versus three configured classes.)

>> Tied scores contribute together

For truth (1,0,1,0) and scores (.9,.8,.8,.1), positive-negative comparisons give
three wins and one tie: AUC=3.5/4=.875. Average precision is
1*(1/2)+(2/3)*(1/2)=5/6. Both tied .8 observations enter at the same threshold.
The resulting scores summarize rankings on four fixed observations; they are not a
model-validation study and do not justify choosing a classifier from this tiny sample.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/metrics_ranking.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const std::vector<int>    truth = {1, 0, 1, 0};
  const std::vector<double> score = {.9, .8, .8, .1};
  const double auc = dm::nn::roc_auc(truth, score), ap = dm::nn::average_precision(truth, score);
  if (std::abs(auc - .875) > 1e-14 || std::abs(ap - 5. / 6) > 1e-14)
    throw std::runtime_error("ranking reference mismatch");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column("truth", std::vector<double>{1, 0, 1, 0});
  t.add_column("score", score);
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.points({0, 2}, {.9, .8}, "positive", {37, 99, 235}, 6)
      .points({1, 3}, {.8, .1}, "negative", {220, 38, 38}, 6)
      .title("One tied positive-negative pair")
      .x_label("fixed observation index")
      .y_label("ranking score")
      .y_limits(0, 1.3);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  p.size(900, 430).save_svg("build/metrics_ranking.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\nROC AUC: {:.9f}; average precision: {:.9f}.\n", t.to_md(3), auc, ap);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| truth | score |
| ---: | ---: |
| 1 | 0.9 |
| 0 | 0.8 |
| 1 | 0.8 |
| 0 | 0.1 |


ROC AUC: 0.875000000; average precision: 0.833333333.
\image(figures/metrics_ranking.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Pairwise half credit for ties and grouped threshold increments give independently checkable ranking scores.)
\alttext(Positive and negative score points are plotted by observation index, including two tied at point eight. A table preserves all labels/scores and reports AUC and average precision.)

> Checking the conventions

>> Batch aggregation and top-score ties

Batch mean losses one and four, from batches of five and two observations, combine
to 13/7, not 2.5. WeightedMean retains that sample weighting. Separately, one score row
(.5,.5,0) with true label one has two equally maximal classes. Argmax accuracy chooses
the first maximum. The native top-k routine counts strictly higher scores, so a true
class tied at the maximum passes top-k even at k=1. This convention can admit more than
k tied classes and therefore need not equal argmax accuracy at k=1.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/metrics_aggregation.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::nn::WeightedMean mean;
  mean.add(1, 5);
  mean.add(4, 2);
  const auto scores   = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{.5, .5, 0}, {1, 3}),
             label    = dm::nn::from_vector(std::vector<std::int64_t>{1});
  const double argmax = dm::nn::accuracy(scores, label),
               top    = dm::nn::top_k_accuracy(scores, label, 1);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame t;
  t.add_column(
      "metric",
      std::vector<std::string>{
          "sample-weighted loss", "unweighted batch loss", "argmax accuracy", "strict-rank top-1"});
  t.add_column("value", std::vector<double>{mean.value(), 2.5, argmax, top});
  auto loss = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), tie = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  loss.bars({0, 1}, {mean.value(), 2.5}, "loss mean", {37, 99, 235}, .5)
      .title("Unequal batches require sample weights")
      .x_label("0 = sample weighted, 1 = batch mean")
      .y_label("aggregated loss")
      .y_limits(0, 3.5);
  tie.bars({0, 1}, {argmax, top}, "accuracy", {220, 38, 38}, .5)
      .title("A top-score tie exposes the convention")
      .x_label("0 = argmax, 1 = strictly-higher rank")
      .y_label("one-row score")
      .y_limits(0, 1.5);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(loss).add(tie).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/metrics_aggregation.svg");
  std::print("{}\n\nWeighted sample count: {}.\n", t.to_md(9), mean.count());
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| metric | value |
| :--- | ---: |
| sample-weighted loss | 1.857142857 |
| unweighted batch loss | 2.500000000 |
| argmax accuracy | 0.000000000 |
| strict-rank top-1 | 1.000000000 |


Weighted sample count: 7.
\image(figures/metrics_aggregation.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Metric names alone do not specify aggregation weights or tie-breaking policies.)
\alttext(One panel compares sample-weighted loss with the average of batch means. Another compares argmax and strict-rank top-one accuracy on a tied score row. The table reports all four values.)

> Conclusion

Counts, declared class sets, ranking ties and aggregation weights determine the score.
The examples compute exact values before applying those metrics to a trained model.
They do not identify a universally best metric, classifier, operating threshold or
probability-calibration policy.

> References

- scikit-learn developers. [ROC AUC|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.roc_auc_score.html] and [average precision|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.metrics.average_precision_score.html]. Canonical binary-ranking definitions and non-interpolated AP.
- TensorFlow authors. [top_k|https://www.tensorflow.org/api_docs/python/tf/math/top_k]. Compare tie policies explicitly; the strict-rank top-k implementation shown here is defined by its native contract.
